---
/*
  NEW. The current drop, and it follows him automatically (2026-08-04).

  WHY IT EXISTS AS ITS OWN ROUTE: a drop is the brand's unit of release, so a
  visitor arriving from Instagram after a launch wants THE NEW THING, not a list
  of drops to choose from. /new is that, permanently, and it needs no code when
  the next drop lands: the current drop is simply the FIRST in his own drag
  order in the studio, so reordering the collections in Sanity is what publishes
  a new one.

  DROP LEFT THE MENU for the same reason. This page reaches the drop index at
  its foot, and each Creature reaches its own drop; the index returns to the
  menu when there are five or six drops rather than three.

  WHAT IT CARRIES: his statement whole (never an excerpt, because this is the
  page the statement is FOR), then the drop's pieces, then the way to the other
  drops.

  THE PIECES ARE NOT SEVENTEEN FULL SCREENS ANY MORE (2026-08-13, section 109).

  They were, and it made this page 19,886px at 1920 — a screen per piece, for
  the whole drop. **This site had already decided that was wrong and this page
  had not been told.** The drop's own page caps its narrative at five, and
  section 42 wrote the reason down: *five full screens is a chapter a reader
  finishes, fifteen is a catalogue they scroll past.* /new was showing
  seventeen.

  So it takes the rhythm section 107 measured off both references: a few
  full-bleed frames BRACKETING a dense two-across mosaic, rather than one
  sustained treatment for the whole run. Two pieces open it at full bleed, the
  middle runs two across, one piece closes it at full bleed. The cover above is
  already the opening atmospheric image, so the pieces need only enough full
  bleed to establish that these are objects seen closely before a reader is
  asked to compare them — and the page ends on a photograph rather than on a
  half-empty grid row.

  A SMALL DROP KEEPS THE NARRATIVE. Five or fewer and every piece stays full
  bleed: at that length the chapter is the right form and a mosaic of four is a
  grid pretending to be one.

  THE MOSAIC IS TWO DEVICES THIS SITE ALREADY OWNS, not a third. The geometry
  is /process's and /about's — full-bleed cells, no gutter, alternating heights
  on a phone and level at desktop so rows line up. The name and price sit in a
  `px-caption` block UNDER each cell, which is exactly how the catalogue does a
  contained tile; putting them through the caption slot would give a half-width
  frame the page's own margins, which is the bug section 56's note describes.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import MediaSurface from "../../components/MediaSurface.astro";
import Statement from "../../components/Statement.astro";
import {
  byStage,
  getCollections,
  getGarmentsInCollection,
  getSiteSettings,
  type Collection,
  type Garment,
} from "../../lib/content";
import {LOCALES, localePath, pick, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {voiceState} from "../../lib/voice";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const collections = await getCollections();
  const current = collections[0] ?? null;
  const garments = current ? await getGarmentsInCollection(current.slug) : [];
  const others = collections.length - 1;
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({
    params: {lang},
    props: {collection: current, garments, others},
  }));
}

const {collection, garments: unordered, others} = Astro.props as {
  collection: Collection | null;
  garments: Garment[];
  others: number;
};
const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const settings = await getSiteSettings();

const garments = byStage(unordered).filter((g) => g.media?.[0]);
const statement = pick(collection?.statement ?? null, locale);
const voice = voiceState(settings, locale);
const cover = collection?.cover ?? null;

const money = new Intl.NumberFormat(locale === "it" ? "it-IT" : "en-GB", {
  style: "currency",
  currency: "EUR",
  maximumFractionDigits: 0,
});
const priceOf = (garment: Garment) =>
  typeof garment.price === "number" ? money.format(garment.price) : null;

/*
  THE BRACKET (section 109). Two full-bleed pieces, the middle two across, one
  full-bleed piece to close. Below six the whole drop stays a chapter.

  The numbers are deliberate and small: the cover is already an atmospheric
  full screen, so two openers are enough to set the scale of the work, and one
  closer is enough to end on a photograph. Change them here and nowhere else.
*/
/*
  AN ANNOUNCEMENT, NOT A SECOND SHOP (2026-08-12, section 113).

  This page showed EVERY piece in the drop, which made it a duplicate of the
  catalogue one click away — the same seventeen garments, the same names, the
  same prices, in a different arrangement. Two pages doing one job, and the
  weaker of the two was the one the menu points at first.

  So it announces. His statement, a HANDFUL of frames, the film of him working,
  and one route into the catalogue for anyone who wants the rest. Six is the
  number: enough to show what the drop is, few enough that nobody mistakes it
  for the shop. Rick Owens' chapter covers carry a name and a verb and nothing
  else; the browsing happens somewhere else, and that is the shape being copied.

  The rest are not hidden — they are one link away, and the link says how many.
*/
const SHOWN = 6;
const shown = garments.slice(0, SHOWN);
const rest = Math.max(0, garments.length - shown.length);
const opening = shown.slice(0, 2);
const middle = shown.slice(2);

/*
  THE FILM BELONGS HERE (2026-08-12, section 113), his instruction. It is the
  one moving thing on the site and it shows him working — which is what a drop
  announcement is for. It is the same clip /process carries, taken from
  `processMedia` by looking for the frame that HAS a video rather than by
  position, so re-ordering that array in the studio cannot silently empty this.

  It stays marked provisional wherever it appears: it is a 464px messaging-app
  copy and the camera original is still the ask.
*/
const film = (settings.processMedia ?? []).find((m) => m.videoUrl) ?? null;
---

<Base
  locale={locale}
  title={collection?.name ?? t(locale, "newDrop")}
  description={statement ?? undefined}
  image={cover ? imageUrl(cover.poster, 1200) : null}
>
  {
    cover ? (
      <MediaSurface media={cover} locale={locale} priority>
        <h1 class="type-display text-display">{collection?.name}</h1>
      </MediaSurface>
    ) : (
      <TextSurface class="flex min-h-svh flex-col justify-center">
        <h1 class="type-display text-display">{t(locale, "newDrop")}</h1>
      </TextSurface>
    )
  }

  {/*
    His statement WHOLE. Every other placement on the site is an excerpt we
    chose; this is the page it was written for.
  */}
  <Statement text={statement} locale={locale} voice={voice} />

  {/* THE OPENING, full bleed: the drop looked at, in his own drag order. */}
  {
    opening.map((garment) => (
      <a href={localePath(locale, `creature/${garment.slug}`)} class="block">
        <MediaSurface media={garment.media![0]} locale={locale} height="tall">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-u1">
            <h2 class="type-label text-label">{garment.name}</h2>
            {priceOf(garment) && <span class="type-mono text-mono">{priceOf(garment)}</span>}
          </div>
        </MediaSurface>
      </a>
    ))
  }

  {/*
    THE MOSAIC. Two across at desktop, one column on a phone, no gutter — the
    arrangement /process and /about use, which is where the density the
    references have actually comes from: more photographs visible at once,
    not less space between them.

    The name and price sit UNDER the frame rather than in its caption slot,
    because a half-width frame taking the page's own margins is a garment's
    name wrapping inside 126px (section 56's note on the worn band). `px-caption`
    is the same edge distance a caption on the picture would have used, so the
    two treatments agree at the edge.
  */}
  {middle.length > 0 && (
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2">
      {middle.map((garment, index) => (
        <a href={localePath(locale, `creature/${garment.slug}`)} class="block">
          <MediaSurface
            media={garment.media![0]}
            locale={locale}
            height={index % 2 === 0 ? "tall" : "short"}
            desktopHeight="tall"
            sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 50vw, 100vw"
          />
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-u1 px-caption py-u2">
            <h2 class="type-label text-label">{garment.name}</h2>
            {priceOf(garment) && <span class="type-mono text-mono">{priceOf(garment)}</span>}
          </div>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  )}

  {/*
    THE FILM (2026-08-12, section 113). Full bleed, no caption, no words: it is
    him working, and a line explaining that would be the site narrating his own
    hands. It closes the announcement the way a photograph does, and it is the
    only moving thing a visitor meets before the catalogue.
  */}
  {film && <MediaSurface media={film} locale={locale} height="tall" />}

  {/*
    u8, not u13 (2026-08-13, section 108). Thirteen units above and below a
    closing block of one or two 12px lines measured 313px at 1920 for 26px of
    content — 92% air around the one thing on the page asking to be clicked.
    Same correction section 105 made to the home page's headings and section
    108 made to /process; the tails were missed because nobody scrolls past the
    end of a page to look at it.
  */}
  <TextSurface class="hairline-h flex flex-col gap-u3 py-u8">
    {/*
      SAY HOW MANY ARE NOT HERE (section 113). This page shows six of the drop;
      the link is the only way to the other eleven, so it has to be worth
      pressing. A count is a fact, in the register facts take, and it is the
      same device the drop page already uses at its own foot.
    */}
    {rest > 0 && (
      <p class="type-mono text-mono">
        {garments.length} {t(locale, "countCreatures")}
      </p>
    )}
    <a
      href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
      class="type-label text-label self-start hairline-under"
    >
      {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
    </a>
    {/*
      The drop index lives here rather than in the menu: it is where a reader
      goes AFTER the current drop, not instead of it.
    */}
    {others > 0 && (
      <a
        href={localePath(locale, "collections")}
        class="type-label text-label self-start hairline-under"
      >
        {t(locale, "otherDrops")}
      </a>
    )}
  </TextSurface>
</Base>
